    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper examines the seasonality of Japanese colonial border security on the Yalu River dividing Korea and Manchuria from 1931 to 1945. With the changing seasons came changing threats to border security--from summer floods that consumed local communities to the winter ice that provided a convenient pathway for smugglers, bandits, and anti-Japanese guerrillas. Border policing required an intimate knowledge of the Yalu landscape and the comparative advantages and disadvantages offered by its seasonally changing forms. Studies of historical borders have largely focused on the discursive, diplomatic, and ethnic rather than environmental aspects of border demarcation and policing.  Examining the Yalu border as a physical space rather than simply an imaginary boundary, however, is critical to understanding the limits of colonial power and contours of everyday life in this frontier region.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
